      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "That line is slow because it's the *first* point where any actual data gets touched \u2014 everything before it (config, scalers) was just metadata.\n\nLooking at the batch shape it prints: `ts: (2, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)` \u2014 batch_size=2, 13 channels, 2 timesteps, full 4096\u00d74096 SDO frames. To build that one batch, the loader has to fetch **4 separate full-resolution NetCDF files** (2 samples \u00d7 2 timesteps each from `time_delta_input_minutes: [-120, -60]`).\n\nPer `config_script.yaml`'s own comments, your `s3_mode: download` setting means each file gets fetched whole from S3 into `s3_cache_dir` before it can be opened \u2014 \"~9s from a warm cache,\" and the config budgets **~1GB per unique timestep**. So this single `next(iter(...))` call can mean several GB pulled over the network on a cold cache, decoded, and then signum-log/z-score normalized on huge arrays \u2014 all real work that config loading and scaler loading never triggered.\n\nOn top of that, since `num_workers=4` here, `build_helio_dataloaders()` uses `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"` (see `builders.py:152`) \u2014 spawn re-imports the whole Python process (torch, lightning, etc.) in each worker rather than forking, which adds real startup latency of its own before the first sample even begins loading.\n\nTwo practical implications:\n- Every notebook up to this cell is cheap (just YAML + small scaler files); this is genuinely the first \"real\" I/O step.\n- If your `s3_cache_dir` persists across runs, re-running this same cell later (or in later notebooks reusing the same cached files) should be much faster \u2014 you're paying the cold-fetch cost once.",
