    {
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "That reflects a real change in `flare_dataset.py` (`downstream_apps/flare_forecast/datasets/flare_dataset.py`) since the version I described earlier \u2014 the dict now has more keys because `load_forecast_frames` is being passed as `True` to the base `HelioNetCDFDataset`, not `False` as the class docstring still claims.\n\nKey by key:\n\n- **`ts`** `(2, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)` \u2014 the input stack: 2 samples, 13 channels, 2 input timesteps (`time_delta_input_minutes: [-120, -60]`), full 4096\u00d74096 frames.\n- **`time_delta_input`** `(2, 2)` \u2014 the actual offsets (in hours) of those 2 input frames, per sample.\n- **`forecast`** `(2, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)` \u2014 this is **not** the flare label. It's `HelioNetCDFDataset`'s own target frame: a full 13-channel Surya image at 1 future timestep (matching `time_delta_target_minutes: 0`). It only appears because `flare_dataset.py:58` does `kwargs.setdefault(\"load_forecast_frames\", True)` \u2014 the code comment right above it (and the class docstring) says this should default to `False` since \"flare forecasting supplies its own labels,\" but the actual call sets `True`. That looks like a bug/leftover from editing, and it means the dataloader is now fetching and holding one *extra* full-resolution frame per sample it doesn't otherwise need \u2014 worth checking if that's intentional, since it also adds to the slow-load issue from your last question.\n- **`lead_time_delta`** `(2, 1)` \u2014 lead time (hours) of that `forecast` frame, from the base class.\n- **`ground_truth`** `(2,)` \u2014 the actual flare-forecasting label: the (optionally log-transformed) flare intensity matched from the HEK catalog. It was deliberately renamed from `forecast` to `ground_truth` (see the comment at `flare_dataset.py:140-143`) specifically because `forecast` was already claimed by the base class's target-frame key above \u2014 a naming collision that would otherwise silently overwrite the real label with an image array.\n- **`ds_index`** \u2014 list of ISO-format timestamps from the flare catalog, one per sample, showing which catalog event each sample was matched to.\n- **`was_flipped`** `(2,)` \u2014 bool per sample indicating whether the vertical-flip augmentation (`flip_probability`) was applied to that sample's `ts`/`forecast`.\n\nSo: `ts` is what you feed the model, `ground_truth` is what you're trying to predict (flare intensity), and `forecast`/`lead_time_delta` are base-class fields that are currently being loaded but appear unused by the flare task \u2014 likely worth setting `load_forecast_frames=False` again unless you specifically need that future frame.",
