    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "34f48781-8062-45fe-8445-236a8dae0ff1",
      "body": "Found it \u2014 the error is attached to that exact cell in the notebook (`cell_id d7d97841`, execution_count 22):\n\n```\nRuntimeError: DataLoader worker (pid(s) 4186, 4204, 4222, 4240) exited unexpectedly\n```\n\n**What this means:** those 4 pids are your `num_workers=4` DataLoader workers. This isn't an error from your code at all \u2014 PyTorch is telling you the worker *processes themselves* died (most likely killed by the OS, e.g. an OOM kill) sometime **before** this cell ran, and this cell is just the first place that tried to talk to them again and got silence. That's why there's no Python traceback pointing at a line in your code \u2014 the main process never got a real exception back, just \"the pipe went dead.\"\n\n**Why now, on the *second* `next(iter(train_data_loader))` call:** `build_helio_dataloaders()` sets `persistent_workers=True` (`builders.py`), so those same 4 workers spawned back at cell `48bb2912` were meant to stay alive and be reused here rather than respawned. Between that cell and this one you loaded the model, ran several intermediate cells, etc. \u2014 plenty of time/memory pressure for the OS to reap those idle worker processes.\n\n**Most likely root cause: memory.** Each worker is holding full-resolution 4096\u00d74096\u00d713-channel arrays. Recall from the last question: because of the `load_forecast_frames=True` default in `flare_dataset.py:58`, every sample now materializes **both** `ts` (2 timesteps) **and** `forecast` (1 timestep) at full res \u2014 roughly 2.6 GB/sample \u00d7 `batch_size=2` \u00d7 `prefetch_factor` (default 2) \u00d7 4 workers. That adds up fast, and a worker that gets OOM-killed just vanishes \u2014 exactly this error, with no stack trace.\n\nThings to try, in order of likely payoff:\n1. **Fix the `load_forecast_frames` bug** \u2014 pass `load_forecast_frames=False` explicitly when building the flare dataloaders (or fix the `setdefault` in `flare_dataset.py:58`), since the flare task doesn't need the extra target frame at all. This roughly halves per-sample memory.\n2. **Restart the kernel** before re-running \u2014 the current `train_data_loader`'s persistent workers are dead; re-running the cell that builds it will spawn fresh ones, but if the memory pressure that killed them is still the issue, it'll happen again.\n3. **Drop `num_workers`** to 1\u20132 in the notebook (it's already lower than the training script's default \"for notebooks start faster\" \u2014 go lower still while debugging), which reduces how many full-res copies are in flight at once.\n4. Watch `free -h` while re-running the cell \u2014 if `available` drops toward 0 right as it dies, that confirms OOM.\n\nWant me to fix the `load_forecast_frames` default in `flare_dataset.py` now, or would you rather just retest with `num_workers` lowered first to confirm the theory?",
      "time": 1789502704.8300555,
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "id": "ae4f73c9-b1d6-4500-8b72-45982bb9a75b",
